WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.270
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:09.170
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; We make eye

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:11.840
contact and the music starts.

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:16.030
he approaches me and
invites me with his left hand.

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:18.970
I accept and step into his frame.

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:25.210
We begin to sway gently to the beat,
getting used to our own movements,

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:26.410
before we take some steps.

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:30.010
He steps back, making room for
me to step forward,

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:35.160
but then he stops, redirecting my
energy to come back around into a turn.

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:38.400
That was fun, and we're warming up.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:43.060
So, he tries again, but this time he
leaves his hand on my head as I keep

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:47.070
spinning, until he decides that he wants
me to stop, and he brings it down.

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:54.160
As those of you who know me know,
I love to partner dance.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:58.900
&gt;From salsa and bachata,
to ballroom and even swing.

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:02.840
I've spent the past ten years learning,
practicing and

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:07.190
dancing around the world.

00:01:07.190 --> 00:01:10.880
One of my favorite parts about partner
dancing is the ability to connect with

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:15.512
people from all walks of life,
using only base level human language.

00:01:15.512 --> 00:01:20.980
Eye contact and touch, and yet
coming together in a flow,

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:25.500
but it can only describe as
both meditative and energizing.

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:28.140
When I dance,

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:32.870
I understand what they mean when they say
the the sum is greater that its parts.

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:38.589
You see, I can move on my own,
my partner can move on his own, but

00:01:38.589 --> 00:01:43.752
then together we can do moves that
neither of us could fully do on our own.

00:01:46.672 --> 00:01:48.530
I want to share with
you a different dance.

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:54.110
This time the dance floor
is a conference room, and

00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:58.000
the leader whose moves I'm following
is a VP at the company that I work for.

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:02.316
This dance takes place over
a series of emails and meetings.

00:02:02.316 --> 00:02:06.456
And one day everything is going great and
we're on the same beat and then, the next

00:02:06.456 --> 00:02:10.116
day it feels as if I were bending in
the middle of the turn left wobbling and

00:02:10.116 --> 00:02:11.626
trying to regain my balance.

00:02:11.626 --> 00:02:14.018
Sounds familiar?

00:02:14.018 --> 00:02:16.530
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You see,

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:20.000
through dance I have learned
more than just dance moves.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:24.170
In learning how to partner dance,
I have learned more about leadership and

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:26.870
team dynamics than in any other class or
book.

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:32.950
So, today my goal is to share with you
the two best practices that I have learned

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:36.923
that can have the biggest impact
on your partners and your dancing,

00:02:36.923 --> 00:02:39.502
with the hope that even
if you don't dance,

00:02:39.502 --> 00:02:42.804
you can use these insights to
be a better leader at work.

00:02:45.588 --> 00:02:50.810
The first big takeaway is that
you should use energy wisely.

00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:57.270
Dance, like most things in life,
is an act of focus energy and

00:02:57.270 --> 00:03:01.770
like our friendly physicist explain,
energy cannot be created nor

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:04.660
destroyed, only transferred or
transformed.

00:03:05.710 --> 00:03:09.750
The same applies in leading and following,
whether they're on the dance floor or

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:14.780
in the conference room, we're always
managing, directing, absorbing and

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:15.670
sharing energy.

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:19.270
Even when we're not aware
that this is happening.

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:23.420
Perhaps, this is why often unaware and

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:27.080
maybe more distant connected leaders
accidentally step on your toes.

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:31.936
They're not aware of where their
energy's going, let alone where it is

00:03:31.936 --> 00:03:35.896
in relations to your's and before you
know it [NOISE], there goes your big toe.

00:03:35.896 --> 00:03:37.449
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So,

00:03:37.449 --> 00:03:43.230
I think that leaders should manage not
just numbers and targets, but also energy.

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:46.110
Let me illustrate this with an example.

00:03:47.710 --> 00:03:49.920
I used to work at Google and

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:55.010
at Google unlike most companies engineers
nominate themselves for a promotion.

00:03:55.010 --> 00:03:55.590
The reason for

00:03:55.590 --> 00:04:00.020
this is that progress in engineering can
sometimes be hard to spot externally.

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:03.650
So, the onus is on the individual to
nominate him or herself when he or

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:04.260
she feels ready.

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:06.000
Seems super fair.

00:04:07.490 --> 00:04:10.810
While the issue was that women
were not nominating themselves for

00:04:10.810 --> 00:04:13.760
a promotion at the same rates
that their male colleagues were.

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:18.540
So, imagine you're a leader at Google,
what do you do about this?

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:25.720
Allen Ustice was a leader in the engineer
organization when he got this information.

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:30.290
And he was really wise, what he did was
he used his position to strategically

00:04:30.290 --> 00:04:32.070
redirect the energy in the organization.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:36.550
He drafted an email,
where he cited academic studies

00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:39.810
that show that girls don't raise their
hands as often in the classroom.

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:43.050
That women don't speak up
as often in meetings and

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:46.390
he said that the same thing
was happening with promotions.

00:04:46.390 --> 00:04:51.070
He sent this to every engineer at Google
and he told the women to advocate for

00:04:51.070 --> 00:04:51.660
themselves.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:57.422
You see, in realizing where the energy
was stuck in the organization,

00:04:57.422 --> 00:05:02.404
Alan was able to use his own leadership
to pivot them, to change their behavior.

00:05:05.388 --> 00:05:06.800
There are many ways to do this.

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:09.260
You can do a more mobile
approach like Allen did.

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:11.780
You can use even more subtle things,
for example,

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:16.600
if you find that your company is being
very siloed you can organize for random

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:20.230
desk Fridays where everyone switches
seats and therefore talks to each other.

00:05:20.230 --> 00:05:22.940
Or If you notice that everyone
is really burned out,

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:24.396
you can give everyone a paid day off.

00:05:24.396 --> 00:05:29.440
The key thing now I want to encourage you
to do, is to use your position as a leader

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:34.010
to direct the energy in the organization,
much like you would in a dance.

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:42.660
The second take away, is that the best
leaders know and adapt to their people.

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:43.630
Not the other way around.

00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:48.840
We have this narrative in the world that
leadership is all about command, and

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:51.360
control, and strength, and rigidity.

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:53.715
And that is exactly the worst
thing in a dance partner.

00:05:53.715 --> 00:05:57.300
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You see, the people who are commanding

00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:00.890
and controlling, they're the ones that
just pull you off of your foot and

00:06:00.890 --> 00:06:02.500
then, you fall or run into someone else.

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:06.590
The best leaders are the ones
who adapt themselves to you.

00:06:06.590 --> 00:06:08.640
If you're really short, they go down.

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:10.620
If you're really tall, they go up.

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:11.350
They adapt.

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:17.200
It's not just about mechanically
being able to do the step perfectly.

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:19.710
It's about how you do it, with others.

00:06:21.290 --> 00:06:24.210
To put it in techie terms, good leaders

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:28.715
are like a website that looks one way
on desktop and different on mobile.

00:06:28.715 --> 00:06:34.180
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Responsive leadership,

00:06:34.180 --> 00:06:35.222
responsive design.

00:06:35.222 --> 00:06:41.920
[LAUGH]
So, how does it actually work in practice?

00:06:42.940 --> 00:06:47.590
So, the best designers can tell you,
good design comes from good insights,

00:06:47.590 --> 00:06:48.390
good people insights.

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:52.870
And we do this in business, in marketing,
in strategy, in sales, we look for

00:06:52.870 --> 00:06:55.410
people insights before we make decisions.

00:06:55.410 --> 00:06:56.590
But why not in leadership?

00:06:58.642 --> 00:07:02.926
Here's another example, this time from
Project Loon, which was Google access

00:07:02.926 --> 00:07:06.400
ambitious project to send Internet
up into the air via balloons.

00:07:07.890 --> 00:07:09.312
To share, how a leader might do this?

00:07:09.312 --> 00:07:14.035
Astro Teller was leading
the project at the time of launch.

00:07:14.035 --> 00:07:18.106
And he had a big group of engineers, and
scientists, everyone's working really

00:07:18.106 --> 00:07:21.952
hard, really excited to finally launch
this project, it's taken so long.

00:07:23.890 --> 00:07:27.346
But there are many potential
complications with this launch and

00:07:27.346 --> 00:07:29.655
he finds himself in a difficult position.

00:07:31.010 --> 00:07:33.520
Fear Astro, like,
how could you mitigate that risk?

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:38.850
You could delay launch and
run a bunch of tests, but

00:07:38.850 --> 00:07:42.420
at the time it's no longer possible,
you're launching in three months.

00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:42.960
So, what do you do?

00:07:45.140 --> 00:07:47.670
Astro realized that he had
a team full of engineers and

00:07:47.670 --> 00:07:53.200
scientists who had become successful
by succeeding in academia.

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:57.360
They had taken lots of tests,
not only were they expert test takers, but

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:00.870
they were proud of the fact that
they were good at taking tests.

00:08:00.870 --> 00:08:05.140
So, he used this insight to
create an activity for them.

00:08:05.140 --> 00:08:09.940
He brought them on to room, gave them each
a piece of paper and pen, set a timer for

00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:13.480
30 minutes and
told everyone you're taking a test.

00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:17.980
Whoever get's the best answer get's
the best grade, you have 30 minutes,

00:08:17.980 --> 00:08:19.860
you only have to answer one question.

00:08:20.930 --> 00:08:24.190
Imagine it's launch day and
everything goes wrong.

00:08:25.250 --> 00:08:25.750
Why?

00:08:27.530 --> 00:08:30.330
Engineers furiously scribble down on the
paper all the reasons why everything was

00:08:30.330 --> 00:08:31.570
going to go wrong.

00:08:31.570 --> 00:08:34.470
And before you knew it, in 30 minutes they
had a huge list of everything that they

00:08:34.470 --> 00:08:37.970
needed to fix to ensure a smooth launch,
but they still had three months to go.

00:08:39.010 --> 00:08:41.450
So, they did it and
they launched flawlessly.

00:08:41.450 --> 00:08:46.910
The big takeaway here is to be responsive
to the people that you're trying to lead,

00:08:46.910 --> 00:08:51.824
adapt yourself to them and then,
you're able to make the best out of them.

00:08:54.819 --> 00:08:59.538
To the untrained eye it seems like the
most technical dancer would be the best

00:08:59.538 --> 00:09:01.560
dance partner.

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:02.740
But, that's simply not true.

00:09:03.880 --> 00:09:08.350
The best dance partners, and
research shows this too, are the people

00:09:08.350 --> 00:09:13.590
who are able to manage energy effectively,
their own and the energy of others.

00:09:13.590 --> 00:09:16.440
The people who are responsive
to the behavior of other people.

00:09:17.580 --> 00:09:20.560
The best leaders have the highest
emotional intelligence, or EQ.

00:09:22.180 --> 00:09:24.710
So, the next time, that you find
yourself in a leadership position,

00:09:24.710 --> 00:09:29.670
I encourage you to hear more than
the music, learn more than steps.

00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:36.360
Hear, see, be, dance with the people
that you're trying to lead.

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:41.030
Not only will you spare their toes, but

00:09:41.030 --> 00:09:45.419
you will also bring the most out of
the energy that each individual has.

00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:50.875
And that my friends is how you
get a guaranteed second dance.

00:09:50.875 --> 00:09:52.980
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] [APPLAUSE]

00:09:52.980 --> 00:09:57.349
[MUSIC]

